<Base title="About the data">
<div class="wrap">
<h1>About the data</h1>
<p class="sub">Where the figures in the country extremes dashboard and games come from, and the licence they are shared under. Compiled 29 October 2026.</p>

<section>
<h2>What the data is</h2>
<p>One row per country or territory on the ISO 3166-1 list (249 codes) plus Kosovo in the games. Each row holds the highest and lowest point, average elevation, record hottest and coldest temperatures, average annual rainfall and average temperatures. Every figure carries a status tag (clean, shared, disputed, unnamed, span mismatch) so it can be used for teaching.</p>
<p>The figures are Wikipedia's. No elevation model or other estimate has been used to fill gaps: if Wikipedia does not give a value, it is left blank.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>Where the figures come from</h2>    <h3>Elevation</h3>
    <p>Highest and lowest points come from the Wikipedia page <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_elevation_extremes_by_country" target="_blank" rel="noopener">List of elevation extremes by country</a>. Average elevations come from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_average_elevation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">List of countries by average elevation</a>.</p>
    <h3>Record temperatures</h3>
    <p>The hottest and coldest temperatures ever recorded in each country come from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_weather_records" target="_blank" rel="noopener">List of weather records</a> on Wikipedia. Where the page lists several records for one country, such as Russia or the United States, the hottest or the coldest is used. Records at shared or disputed locations are left out of the games.</p>
    <h3>Average temperatures</h3>
    <p>Average daily highs, average daily lows and average yearly temperatures cover 1991 to 2020 and come from the <a href="https://climateknowledgeportal.worldbank.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">World Bank Climate Change Knowledge Portal</a>, which uses Climatic Research Unit gridded data (version 4.07). This is the one source that is not Wikipedia.</p>
    <h3>Rainfall</h3>
    <p>Rainfall comes from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_average_annual_precipitation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">List of countries by average annual precipitation</a> on Wikipedia. It is the average over the whole country, not the wettest place in it.</p>
    <h3>Units</h3>
    <p>Figures are shown in metric units by default: metres, degrees Celsius and millimetres. Every game has a Metric or Imperial switch on its start screen for feet, degrees Fahrenheit and inches. Your choice is remembered in your browser and applies to all the games.</p>
    <h3>Global averages</h3>
    <p>The dashed reference line in the answer graphics shows a global figure. Average elevation uses the world figure on Wikipedia (840 metres). Average temperatures and rainfall are the means of the country figures weighted by land area, using the land areas in Wikipedia's <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_and_dependencies_by_area" target="_blank" rel="noopener">List of countries and dependencies by area</a>; they describe land only, and Antarctica has no figure. For highest points, lowest points and record temperatures the line is the plain average of all countries.</p>
    <h3>Countries and maps</h3>
    <p>The games cover the 249 countries and territories on the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_3166-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ISO 3166-1</a> list, plus Kosovo, which has no code on it. A country with no figure in a source is left out of the questions that need that figure. Map outlines come from <a href="https://www.naturalearthdata.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Natural Earth</a>. Wikipedia figures were fetched on 29 October 2026.</p>
  
</section>

<section>
<h2>Licence</h2>
<div class="box"><strong>The data and the games are shared under
<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0)</a>.</strong></div>
<p><strong>Why this licence.</strong> Wikipedia's text is available under CC BY-SA 4.0 and the GFDL; Wikipedia's reuse terms require attribution, a notice of the licence, an indication of changes, and that adapted versions are shared under CC BY-SA 4.0 or later. CC BY-SA 4.0 satisfies all of those, so it is the right licence for a dataset built from Wikipedia's tables. Wikipedia says some text is CC BY-SA only and cannot be reused under the GFDL, so the GFDL is not used here.</p>
<p><strong>What that means for you.</strong> You may copy, share and adapt the data and the games, including commercially, if you:</p>
<ul>
<li>credit the sources below (Wikipedia and its contributors, with a link to the page and revision) and this dataset;</li>
<li>link to the licence;</li>
<li>say what you changed (this dataset cleans units, splits multi-value rows, tags statuses, joins to map shapes and drops records not on the ISO list);</li>
<li>share anything you make from it under CC BY-SA 4.0 or later.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Suggested credit.</strong> "Country extremes, compiled from Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0) and other sources listed at [this page], adapted and licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0."</p>
<p class="muted" style="color:var(--mute);font-size:14px">Copyright protects creative expression, not bare facts, so a single elevation figure is not itself protected. The compiled tables, selection and wording are, which is why the licence is applied to the whole. This is a practical reading of the terms, not legal advice.</p>
</section>

